==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*lewk-}}
From {{bor|en|la|lūcēns|lūcentem}}, the present participle of {{m|la|lūceō|lūcēre|to shine}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈl(j)uːsnt/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-lucent.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-lucent2.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈlus(ə)nt/|a=US}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Emitting light; [[shining]], [[luminous]].
#* {{RQ:T. E. Lawrence Seven Pillars|XXXIX|228|Sherif Nasir led us: his '''lucent''' goodness, which provoked answering devotion even from the depraved, made him the only leader (and a benediction) for forlorn hopes.}}
# [[translucent|Translucent]]; [[clear]], [[lucid]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1891|author=w:Mary Noailles Murfree|chapter=I|title=In the “Stranger People’s” Country|location=New York|publisher=w:Harper & Brothers|oclc=1085336950|page=16
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/strangerpeoples00murfrich/page/16/mode/1up|passage={{...}} her dilated eyes fixed with a horror-stricken fascination upon the pygmy burial-ground, in that broad, '''lucent''' expanse of the yellow moonlight which was still streaming through the illuminated gorge of the mountains into an otherwise dusky world.}}

